# Round 1 Consultation — Monument Acoustics / Cymatics Spec
**Scope:** computation-ready modal physics for 6 sites; status of the 110 Hz claim; mode-space mapping to circular-membrane (m,n); physical caveats. c = 343 m/s throughout.

---

## 0. Ground rules (validity of what follows)

- Room-mode formula `f = (c/2)·√((nx/Lx)²+(ny/Ly)²+(nz/Lz)²)` assumes a **rigid-walled, empty rectangular box** with pressure-release-free (Neumann) boundaries. Real chambers fail this progressively via: non-rectangular geometry (corbelled vaults, curved ceilings), doorway/aperture losses, surface absorption, and modal overlap.
- **Schroeder frequency** `f_s ≈ 2000·√(RT60/V)` [Hz, V in m³]: above f_s the spectrum is statistical (modal overlap); discrete-mode talk is only defensible below f_s. All sites here have f_s ≈ 250–580 Hz → the 95–120 Hz band is in the **discrete-mode regime** (good for cross-matching), but claims like "King's Chamber resonates at 438 Hz" are physically meaningless (modal density there ≈ 20 modes/Hz).
- Scaling law: f ∝ c/L. All chambers here (L ~ 2.5–10.5 m) have **fundamentals of 15–50 Hz** (infrasonic/low bass). **No chamber's fundamental is 110 Hz.** 110 Hz is a 3rd–9th order axial mode of any room ~3–12 m in size — this matters for §2.
- Open-air sites (Stonehenge, El Castillo) are **not resonators**. They are scatterers/diffractors. Treat them with grating/repetition-pitch physics, not eigenmodes (§1e, §1f).

